
MoM – John’s Key Discussion & Action Items

Key Architectural Principles

* Reuse before creating: If a feature has significant overlap with existing functionality, enhance/reuse the existing shared table, entity, or UI component instead of creating a new one.
* Avoid table/junction sprawl: Consolidate relationships (Goal–Team, Vendor–Tag, Item–Goal, etc.) into a common entity/relationship model where appropriate.
* Establish governance to decide whether a feature should become a first-class entity or use an existing shared entity.
* Shared UI/UX components, notifications, permissions, comments, notes, history, approvals, QA runs, reactions, etc. should be reusable across entities.

Knowledge Base & Permissions

* Implement granular knowledge-base permissions at:
    * Knowledge Base level
    * Hierarchy-node level
    * Individual article level
* Permissions should support actions such as view, create, edit, delete, publish and archive.
* Higher-level permissions should flow down unless overridden at a more specific level.

LangSmith / LangGraph

* LangSmith should be the technical operating/observability layer for LLM calls and evaluation data.
* Start scaffolding LangGraph graphs, subgraphs and nodes.
* Create the relationship between application records and deployed agents/graphs so users can see and execute agent workflows against records.
* Knowledge gaps identified through evaluation should feed the knowledge-content workflow.

Environment & PR/Release Process

* Establish a clear flow: Local Dev → Remote Dev/shared DB → Staging → Production.
* Define both human stage gates and automated GitHub PR tests/checks.
* Production should contain only explicitly approved changes; PRs/features need clear scope and validation before promotion.

Asset & File Management

* Create a common Asset → Files → File Versions model across the monorepo.
* Assets should be polymorphic/reusable, allowing one uploaded document to relate to multiple entities without duplicate uploads.
* Support file versioning (V1, V2, V3…) and consistent underlying storage integration.
* Review how the Knowledge Base currently handles attachments and align it with the common model.

Migration & Cleanup

* During migration, identify and remove dead code, unused tables and obsolete POC functionality.
* Review tables such as LLM Settings and determine whether they are still required or should be replaced by existing LangSmith configuration.
* Where tables have overlapping functionality, collapse/consolidate them rather than maintaining duplicates.
* The goal is to reduce technical debt, simplify the architecture and eliminate potential sources of bugs.

